Author: Danny Milosavljevic <dannym@friendly-machines.com>
Date: 2026-04-20
Subject: Cast the destination's type, not the call node's, in Val/floatdef (fpc 0.99)
License: expat

In compiler/cg386inl.pas the Val(string, destvar[, code]) handler
does, in the `floatdef:` arm:

    if pfloatdef(p^.resulttype)^.typ <> f32bit then
      inc(fpuvaroffset);

`p' is the whole Val call node.  Its resulttype is the procedure's
*return type* (a void-like slot for the Val intrinsic), not the
destination variable's type.  Casting that slot as `pfloatdef' is
wrong: the def pointed at has nothing to do with floatdef and a
read of `.typ' lands at a meaningless offset.

The outer `case dest_para^.resulttype^.deftype of' just confirmed
that `dest_para^.resulttype' really is a floatdef, so that is the
one to cast.  Native FPC happened to tolerate the original cast
because the bytes read by `.typ' held a value that compared equal
to `f32bit' often enough to avoid obvious misbehaviour; under tp2cc
the field read on the wrong record type is rejected at runtime.

--- a/compiler/cg386inl.pas
+++ b/compiler/cg386inl.pas
@@ -732,7 +732,12 @@
              floatdef:
                begin
                   procedureprefix := 'FPC_VAL_REAL_';
-                  if pfloatdef(p^.resulttype)^.typ<>f32bit then
+                  { Upstream wrote pfloatdef(p^.resulttype) -- wrong.  p is
+                    the whole Val call node; its resulttype is the procedure
+                    return type, not the destination's.  The outer case just
+                    confirmed dest_para^.resulttype is a floatdef, so cast
+                    that one. }
+                  if pfloatdef(dest_para^.resulttype)^.typ<>f32bit then
                     inc(fpuvaroffset);
                end;
              orddef:
